  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 242 AM CST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nCurrent satellite water vapor imagery shows lead short wave over \nnorthern New Mexico/southeast Colorado racing northeast. This system \nhas sparked off a line of showers/storms over the Texas panhandle \nand southwest Kansas. This activity will race northeast and reach \nlocations along and west of I-35/I-135 early this morning. The \nstrong wind shear/low CAPE environment could support a few stronger \nwind gusts especially for any organized line segment due to downward \nmixing of low level jet winds to the surface. This line of storms \nwill shift into southeast Kansas where it could uptick a bit in \nintensity this afternoon. The next area we will be watching closely \nfor storm re-development and magnitude of daytime heating is between \n2pm-5pm for locations near or west of highway 14 in central/south \ncentral KS. This is where high resolution models continue to show \nhigher probabilities of storm initiation ahead of upper PV wave \nalong the dry-line. Environment should become more favorable for \nsurface based severe storm potential given favorable combination of \n0-3km CAPE and 0-1km SRH co-located with 40-50kts of effective wind \nshear. This presents a brief window for storm mode to start out semi-\ndiscrete before quickly transitioning to more linear storm mode \ngiven evolution of wind shear vector alignment. The initial stronger \nstorms would capable of producing damaging winds, large hail, along \nwith a risk for a few brief tornadoes into the early evening hours \nfollowed by a transition thereafter to mainly strong-damaging winds \nas the storms become linear. The line of storms looks to shift east \nof southeast Kansas around midnight. \n\nUpper level wave will shift northeast of Kansas on Tuesday with \ndeformation area of wrap around showers ending over central Kansas \nby late morning. The departing upper level wave will push cooler \ndrier air southward along with gusty northwest winds. A dry weather \npattern for the Kansas region seems likely to unfold for Wednesday \nthrough the upcoming weekend. Although models do show another \nweather system ejecting from the Rockies and moving southeast into \nthe southern plains on Thursday. This system will have limited \nmoisture to work with so not expecting much other than perhaps a few \nrain showers over southern Kansas for Thursday. Long range \ndeterministic models are consistently showing a upper-level wave \ndropping southeast across the northern plains, then deepening \nconsiderably with-in a northwest flow regime aloft over Iowa/Illinois \nregion. This current weather system track would keep precipitation \nchances east of Kansas as drier air spreads southeast from Nebraska \ninto Kansas.",
